netstack: SIOCSIFFLAGS

What: ioctl(SIOCSIFFLAGS) sets an interface's flags as Linux's dev_ifsioc()
does through dev_change_flags(): it needs CAP_NET_ADMIN in the user namespace
of the socket's network namespace, checked before the device is looked up
there (EPERM, then ENODEV), takes ifr_flags as the complete set, brings the
interface up or down as RTM_SETLINK already does, ignores the flags that
userspace cannot change, such as IFF_RUNNING, fails with EOPNOTSUPP for a
change of one that netstack does not model, such as IFF_PROMISC, and does not
copy the ifreq back. Before, it failed with ENOTTY. Like it, the interface
ioctls that read (SIOCGIFFLAGS, SIOCGIFINDEX and the others) and SIOCGIFCONF
act in the socket's network namespace, not the caller's, as Linux's
sock_ioctl() passes sock_net() to dev_ioctl().

Why: crun brings the loopback interface of a network namespace it creates up
with SIOCGIFFLAGS and SIOCSIFFLAGS (src/libcrun/linux.c). Buildah's RUN steps
in `podman build` run in such a namespace, so every RUN of a build on Podman's
own network failed: "ioctl(SIOCSIFFLAGS): Inappropriate ioctl for device".
slirp4netns, rootless Podman 4's default network, configures its TAP device
with it too.

Upstream: upstream PR 15559 ("netstack: honor IFF_UP in SIOCSIFFLAGS and
RTM_SETLINK", head b1bde0f1feaa, open and conflicting with master on
2026-11-17) adds the same ioctl; its RTM_SETLINK half is already in
release-20261109.0, on which this patch builds. Drop this patch when upstream
has SIOCSIFFLAGS.

Verified:
- The new syscall test NetdeviceTest.SetInterfaceFlags (lo of a new network
  namespace up, down and up again, also through it from another namespace,
  whose lo stays down; ENODEV; EPERM without CAP_NET_ADMIN)
  passes on Linux 7.0 under runc and on runsc with this patch, under the
  runtime arguments of runsc and of runsc-nested, and fails on ALE's 0.1.2;
  the whole socket_netdevice_test passes (8 tests).
- `podman build` of a Dockerfile with RUN steps on Podman's default network,
  with crun, built in sandboxes under runsc-nested (Podman 4.9.3 on CNI and
  netavark, 5.4.2 and 5.8.9).

